 electron only","Gained a neutron"],"B",5,1,"dea26f0c1a61",0],["A technician handling laboratory chemicals notices that a solution contains H⁺ ions but no OH⁻ ions. The solution is most likely:",["Acidic","Basic","Neutral","Amphoteric"],"A",5,1,"6f7c6110974a",0],["Which substance can behave as both proton donor and proton acceptor depending on conditions?",["HCl","NaOH","H₂O","HNO₃"],"C",5,1,"af24b9c1e030",0],["Which pair consists entirely of bases?",["HCl and HNO₃","H₂SO₄ and H₃PO₄","NaOH and KOH","HCl and NaOH"],"C",5,1,"7bd3a49980b2",0],["A substance accepts H⁺ from another substance and forms a positive ion. It is acting as a:",["Salt","Acid","Base","Catalyst"],"C",5,1,"648f95ea504f",0],["Which observation indicates the presence of an alkali rather than merely a base?",["It reacts with acids","It contains OH⁻ ions","It is soluble in water","It turns red litmus blue"],"C",5,1,"6b92510ba6df",0],["A chemical sample acts as an acid in one reaction and as a base in another. The sample is:",["Neutral","Amphoteric","Metallic","Inert"],"B",5,1,"5304b1c9a504",0],["A chemistry student concludes that a solution is basic because it contains excess OH⁻ ions and changes indicator color accordingly. Which indicator observation supports this conclusion?",["Blue litmus → Red","Red litmus → Blue","Blue litmus → White","Red litmus → Yellow"],"B",5,1,"2765507e3e4e",0],["An unknown oxide reacts with water to produce a solution that turns red litmus blue. The oxide is most likely:",["Acidic oxide","Neutral oxide","Basic oxide","Amphoteric oxide"],"C",5,1,"d742f84a580b",0],["Magnesium oxide is added to water. The resulting solution becomes slightly basic. This occurs because magnesium oxide is a:",["Non-metal oxide","Acidic oxide","Basic oxide","Neutral oxide"],"C",5,1,"19c9de014b9b",0],["When calcium oxide is mixed with water, the resulting solution turns red litmus blue. Which ion is mainly responsible for this behavior?",["H⁺","Cl⁻","OH⁻","Ca²⁺"],"C",5,1,"d8d1fdd1cf26",0],["A stude